//? Title: Booth Multiplication: Signed Digits and Their Actual Cost
//? Subtitle: Visible recoding, exact widened products, long runs and a finite operation-count distribution
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Multiply signed 32-bit integers exactly while exposing how the multiplier's bit
transitions select positive and negative partial terms. Booth recoding can
replace a long run of ones by two active digits, but isolated or alternating
bits can cost more. We inspect a small product, retain the original signed
examples and run-length experiment, and check every signed-byte multiplier's
operation count. The product, counts and accumulator trace share one core loop.
)

\toc

> The method

Let b_i be bit i of the multiplier's fixed 32-bit representation, and set the
implicit lower bit b_-1 to zero. The signed digit is
\(d_i=b_{i-1}-b_i\). Pair order matters: the first bit below is current,
the second previous.

| current b_i | previous b_(i-1) | digit d_i | action |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| 0 | 0 | 0 | no contribution |
| 1 | 1 | 0 | no contribution |
| 1 | 0 | -1 | subtract M times 2^i |
| 0 | 1 | +1 | add M times 2^i |

The recoding telescopes:

$$
\sum_{i=0}^{31}(b_{i-1}-b_i)2^i
=\sum_{i=0}^{30}b_i2^i-b_{31}2^{31}=Q.
$$
\alttext(The signed-digit sum equals the low thirty-one unsigned bit weights minus the sign bit's weight, which is the signed multiplier Q.)

Multiplying this identity by M proves the exact result. After processing bit i,
the recoded prefix is
\(\sum_{j=0}^{i-1}b_j2^j-b_i2^i\), which lies between -2^i and
2^i-1. With int32 M and i at most 31, every scaled term and accumulated value
fits int64. The core widens M and multiplies by a positive power of two;
it does not left-shift a negative operand. There are exactly 32 decisions,
including zeros, and the final sign-bit weight needs no extra step.

> Simple example: 3 times 7

The positive multiplier 7 has low bits 0111, so \(7=2^3-2^0\).
Only digits d0=-1 and d3=+1 are active: the product is -3+24=21. The
diagram reads those terms from the actual trace. Higher multiplier bits are zero.

```{cpp, file="figures/booth_terms.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::booth_multiply_trace(3,7);
  const auto& low=trace.steps[0];const auto& high=trace.steps[3];
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;style.width=3.4;style.height=1.6;
  diagram.node("input",{0,0},"M = 3\nQ = 00000111\n7 = 8 - 1",style);
  style.fill={255,237,213};
  diagram.node("low",{5.4,1.5},std::format("bit {}, pair ({},{})\ndigit {}\nterm {}",
      low.bit_index,low.current,low.previous,low.digit,low.contribution),style);
  style.fill={220,252,231};
  diagram.node("high",{5.4,-1.5},std::format("bit {}, pair ({},{})\ndigit +{}\nterm +{}",
      high.bit_index,high.current,high.previous,high.digit,high.contribution),style);
  style.fill={219,234,254};
  diagram.node("product",{10.8,0},std::format("{} + {}\nproduct {}",low.contribution,high.contribution,trace.result.product),style)
      .edge("input","low","start run").edge("input","high","end run")
      .edge("low","product").edge("high","product")
      .text({5.4,3.2},"Pairs are (current bit, previous bit); all other digits are zero.");
  diagram.save_svg("build/booth_terms.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/booth_terms.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The low eight bits are shown for readability; the native loop processes all 32. Two active signed digits replace three positive one-bit terms in this example.)
\alttext(Three times seven splits into a minus-three contribution at bit zero and a plus-twenty-four contribution at bit three, which sum to twenty-one.)

> Original signed examples and a negative multiplicand trace

The four original products are retained, followed by full-range boundary
controls. Operation names refer to the signed recoding digit. When M is
negative, subtracting a scaled M makes a positive numeric contribution.
For -7 times 6, the active terms are +14 and -56, giving -42.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <limits>
int main() {
  const auto minimum=std::numeric_limits<std::int32_t>::min();
  std::print("| M | Q | native product | widened built-in product | additions | subtractions |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& [m,q]:std::vector<std::pair<std::int32_t,std::int32_t>>{
       {-7,6},{123456,-789},{-1000,-1000},{32767,32767},{minimum,minimum},{minimum,-1}}) {
    const auto result=dm::algorithms::booth_multiply(m,q);
    const auto reference=std::int64_t(m)*q;if (result.product!=reference) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",m,q,result.product,reference,result.additions,result.subtractions);
  }
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::booth_multiply_trace(-7,6);
  std::print("\n| bit i | current | previous | digit | scaled M | contribution | accumulator |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<4;++i) {
    const auto& s=trace.steps[i];
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",s.bit_index,s.current,s.previous,s.digit,
        s.scaled_multiplicand,s.contribution,s.accumulator);
  }
}
```
