> Checking the theory: one run versus alternating bits

A positive run of k low ones is \(2^k-1\), so for 1<=k<=31 it has one
subtraction at bit zero and one addition at bit k. A straightforward positive
binary expansion has k one-bit terms. Thus Booth uses more active digits at
k=1, the same number at k=2, and fewer for longer runs.

A sign-extended negative run behaves differently: Q=-1 has only the initial
subtraction, while the high bits remain one. The positive alternating pattern
0x55555555 has sixteen one bits but thirty-two transitions, so it requires
thirty-two active Booth digits. These counts describe the recoding and do not
measure execution time or establish a hardware speedup.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/booth_runs.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <bit>
#include <limits>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> length,booth,binary;
  for (unsigned k=1;k<=31;++k) {
    const auto q=static_cast<std::int32_t>((std::uint64_t{1}<<k)-1);
    const auto result=dm::algorithms::booth_multiply(1,q);
    const auto count=result.additions+result.subtractions;if (count!=2) return 1;
    length.push_back(k);booth.push_back(count);binary.push_back(std::popcount(std::uint32_t(q)));
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(length,binary,"binary popcount",dm::RGB{220,38,38},2)
      .lines(length,booth,"Booth active digits",dm::RGB{22,163,74},2)
      .title("One positive run: two active Booth digits")
      .x_label("run length k in Q = 2^k - 1").y_label("active terms")
      .y_limits(0,42).size(900,480).save_svg("build/booth_runs.svg");
  std::print("| Q | full 32-bit representation | additions | subtractions | active digits |\n"
             "| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::int32_t q:{0,1,7,0x55555555,-1,std::numeric_limits<std::int32_t>::min()}) {
    const auto result=dm::algorithms::booth_multiply(1,q);
    std::print("| {} | {:032b} | {} | {} | {} |\n",q,std::uint32_t(q),result.additions,result.subtractions,
        result.additions+result.subtractions);
  }
}
```
