| 1 | 00000000000000000000000000000001 | 1 | 1 | 2 |
| 7 | 00000000000000000000000000000111 | 1 | 1 | 2 |
| 1431655765 | 01010101010101010101010101010101 | 16 | 16 | 32 |
| -1 | 11111111111111111111111111111111 | 0 | 1 | 1 |
| -2147483648 | 10000000000000000000000000000000 | 0 | 1 | 1 |
\image(figures/booth_runs.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original run-length sweep is extended from 16 to 31 positive bits. The table adds patterns for which the two-per-run slogan needs qualification.)
\alttext(Binary active-term counts grow from one to thirty-one with run length, while Booth counts remain two. Alternating bits and negative sign extension are listed separately.)

> Checking the theory: exact operation-count distribution

The indicator of an active digit is \(t_i=b_i\mathbin{\mathrm{xor}}b_{i-1}\).
This transform is bijective: starting with b_-1=0, recover each b_i by
\(b_i=t_i\mathbin{\mathrm{xor}}b_{i-1}\). Uniform 32-bit patterns
therefore give a Binomial(32,1/2) active-digit count with mean sixteen. Long-run
savings do not imply a universal reduction in active terms.

For a complete finite check, enumerate all 256 signed-byte values but still use
the native 32-bit routine. Sign extension makes every comparison above bit seven
equal, leaving eight variable transitions. Their exact frequencies must be the
binomial coefficients \(\binom8j\), with mean four active digits.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/booth_distribution.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <array>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::array<unsigned,9> observed{};
  const std::array<unsigned,9> predicted{1,8,28,56,70,56,28,8,1};
  for (int raw=0;raw<256;++raw) {
    const auto result=dm::algorithms::booth_multiply(1,raw<128?raw:raw-256);
    ++observed[result.additions+result.subtractions];
  }
  if (observed!=predicted) return 1;
  std::vector<double> x,actual,theory,zero;
  std::print("| active digits | observed signed-byte multipliers | exact binomial count |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t j=0;j<observed.size();++j) {
    x.push_back(j);actual.push_back(observed[j]);theory.push_back(predicted[j]);zero.push_back(0);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",j,observed[j],predicted[j]);
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .segments(x,zero,x,actual,dm::RGB{37,99,235},3)
      .points(x,actual,"observed",dm::RGB{37,99,235},7)
      .points(x,theory,"exact binomial",dm::RGB{217,119,6},3)
      .title("Active digits over all 256 signed-byte multipliers")
      .x_label("nonzero recoding digits").y_label("number of multiplier values")
      .x_limits(-.5,8.5).y_limits(0,110).size(900,480).save_svg("build/booth_distribution.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| active digits | observed signed-byte multipliers | exact binomial count |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 1 | 1 |
| 1 | 8 | 8 |
| 2 | 28 | 28 |
| 3 | 56 | 56 |
| 4 | 70 | 70 |
| 5 | 56 | 56 |
| 6 | 28 | 28 |
| 7 | 8 | 8 |
| 8 | 1 | 1 |
\image(figures/booth_distribution.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The orange exact predictions fall on the blue observed stem tops. This is exhaustive enumeration of a finite input set, rather than a statistical fit.)
\alttext(The counts for zero through eight active digits are one, eight, twenty-eight, fifty-six, seventy, fifty-six, twenty-eight, eight and one, matching the binomial coefficients.)

Native tests also check all 65,536 signed-byte operand pairs, with every partial
accumulator compared to the signed-prefix identity. A hundred thousand generated
full-width pairs and the existing two-hundred-thousand-pair suite check exact
widened built-in products, including both INT32_MIN operands. Undefined-behavior
sanitizer checks cover the focused suite. Logical counts remain positive for
some recodings even when M=0; the API counts selected signed digits.

> Conclusion

The corrected pair rule, telescoping proof and actual accumulator trace agree
with the original signed products and full-range boundary controls. Long positive
runs give two active digits, while alternating bits supply a counterexample to
unconditional savings. The complete signed-byte distribution confirms the
transition-count derivation.

Both APIs make exactly 32 decisions. The ordinary result uses constant storage;
the trace owns 32 events. This implementation accumulates widened terms rather
than emulating physical accumulator/multiplier registers. It demonstrates the
radix-2 arithmetic, with no hardware timing claim. Generated integer inputs are
sufficient, so no observed dataset is needed.

> References

- Booth, A. D. (1951). *A signed binary multiplication technique*. The Quarterly Journal of Mechanics and Applied Mathematics, 4(2), 236–240. https://doi.org/10.1093/qjmam/4.2.236. The native implementation follows the signed-digit identity with widened accumulation.
